<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Rate of explanations stating that the target is a random series (evaluation result option 4). Upper panel: results with the original prompt (Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Configuration for Regularity Identification in Number Sequences ‣ 3 Method ‣ The Idola Tribus of AI: Large Language Models tend to perceive order where none exists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); lower panel: results with the random-allowing prompt (Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Result ‣ The Idola Tribus of AI: Large Language Models tend to perceive order where none exists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Boldface indicates the highest rate by an individual model for each category.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The success rates with the random-allowing prompt configuration are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4 Result ‣ The Idola Tribus of AI: Large Language Models tend to perceive order where none exists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Compared to Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Result ‣ The Idola Tribus of AI: Large Language Models tend to perceive order where none exists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the improvements correspond to the increase in random-series explanations reported in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Result ‣ The Idola Tribus of AI: Large Language Models tend to perceive order where none exists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
The o3 and o4-mini models showed comparable performance, achieving the highest success rates among the models.
Nevertheless, success rates for quasi-ordered series remain significantly lower than those for their corresponding ordered cases across all models.
Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview Thinking did not show a notable improvement, as the number of random-series explanations did not increase substantially.</p>
</div>
